WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.220
Narrator: Washington debates for the '70s.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:18.270
A series of debates designed to bring together
for an open exchange of views and opinions,

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:23.600
outstanding authorities on vital issues facing
the world of the '70s.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:30.020
The topic, "Social Security: Universal or
selective."

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.770
Now, here is Peter Hackett.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:38.910
Peter Hackes: In our society, people are living longer
than ever before.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.690
Retirement comes earlier than ever before.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:47.350
Hence, our senior citizens are finding many
more years to be lived without the financial

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.940
security of a job, thus an issue of prime
importance to millions of Americans is social

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.480
security.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.719
Despite the fact that social security is a
fact of American life and has been for many

00:00:58.719 --> 00:01:04.289
years since its inception in the depression
of the 1930s, the system has been criticized

00:01:04.289 --> 00:01:06.250
for some shortcomings.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:11.600
Some attacked the very concept of social security
and insist that it be done away with.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.200
Welcome to another in a series of rational
debate seminars presented by the American

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.990
Enterprise Institute for Public Policy Research.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:24.090
A nonprofit, nonpartisan research and educational
organization.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:29.600
Our two debaters are Wilbur Cohen the former
Secretary of Health Education and Welfare

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:34.720
in the Johnson administration, and the noted
economist, Dr. Milton Friedman.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.140
We also have with us a panel of experts in
the field, people engaged in making public

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:43.880
policy through teaching, influencing, and
writing about economic matters.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.030
They will comment upon and question our debaters.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:53.670
Now, to present our discussion, Peter Lisagor,
a longtime Washington newsman who is Washington

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.060
bureau chief for, "The Chicago Daily News,"
Mr. Lisagor.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:07.409
Peter Lisagor: Ladies and gentlemen, welcome
to another in a series of rational debate

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:08.530
seminars.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:13.370
One would have thought that social security
was a program universally accepted by most

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:19.170
Americans and quite beyond the reach of even
a rational debate.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:24.720
Not so of course, and especially not so when
you have two of the liveliest minds in the

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.980
country debating its virtues and its drawbacks.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:35.390
This is the sixth rational debate seminar
of the 1970-71 academic year.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:41.019
Our subject is, "Social security: Universal
or selective."

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:47.860
And our two participants are in the order
in which they will debate on my right, Wilbur

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.980
J. Cohen.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.760
He's a former Secretary of Health Education
and Welfare.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.620
He's now Dean of the School of Education and
professor of education at the University of

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.569
Michigan.

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:06.180
Mr. Cohen became involved in social security
affairs before the inception of the program.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:12.120
In 1934, he was research assistant to the
executive director of President Roosevelt's

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.989
Cabinet Committee on Economic Security, the
committee that drafted the original Social

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:19.970
Security Act.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:25.280
Mr. Cohen is author of several books and numerous
articles in the field of social security,

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.670
health, welfare, and education.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:38.590
And on my left, and don't take this literally,
Milton Friedman, one of the most noted economists

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.460
in the world today.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:46.849
He is the leader of the so-called Chicago
School of Economics known to some as the Cook

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:49.330
County School of Economics.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:54.930
He's been professor of economics at the University
of Chicago since 1948 and is currently the

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.920
Paul Snowden Russell Distinguished Service
professor of economics there.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:07.030
He is a regular columnist for, "Newsweek Magazine,"
and a prolific writer having authored several

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.040
books on economics.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.830
Ladies and gentlemen, first, Mr. Cohen.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:25.530
Wilbur Cohen: I am indeed gratified that we have
this opportunity for a periodic reappraisal

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.160
of the social security system.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.030
There was such a reappraisal in the 1936 campaign
when President Landon attacked the social

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:36.720
security program as being a cruel hoax.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.720
The re-election of President Roosevelt established
for all time that the people of this country

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.270
believed in its principles, and it was extended.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:50.720
In 1953, the program was again reappraised
by a Republican administration under President

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.840
Eisenhower, and in 1954, it was agreed that
the program was sound and desirable in the

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.380
American system and it was again extended.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:06.340
In 1969, it was reappraised again by President
Nixon and his cabinet officers, and it was

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:11.140
found to be a kind of programs that should
be extended.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:15.310
So I believe this fourth reappraisal will
be good and the net result will be another

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:21.520
further extension and improvement of social
security, which Congress will adopt this year.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.370
Social security is a program which works.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:32.830
Twenty-six million people draw benefits each
month, 80 million people contribute to it,

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:38.590
and it is a program which seems to have the
overwhelming support of the 26 million people

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:44.960
who draw the benefits, and as far as taxes
are concerned as much enthusiasm for the payment

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.380
of those taxes as anyone can find.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:55.410
It is of course the most important guaranteed
income program in the United States.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:06:02.210
It provides as far as possible in this world
of doubt and limitation, a guaranteed income

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:11.150
to the people who are under it by providing
for a mechanism of insurance, a trust fund,

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:19.070
and kinds of taxes which enable the body politic
to believe, sincerely, and the political people

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:24.740
to believe that they have done as best they
can in this rather fallible world of ours,

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:29.960
to provide a guarantee to the beneficiaries
who will receive it.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:35.330
In addition, it is the biggest anti-poverty
program in the United States.

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:40.130
Among the 26 million beneficiaries, there
are 11 million whom if there were no social

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:46.040
security would now be in the poverty program.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:52.729
It is a program which of course by demonstration
appeals to all of the various income and other

00:06:52.729 --> 00:06:54.819
groups in our society.

00:06:54.819 --> 00:07:00.860
It appeals to the low income groups because
it does provide income to low-income people,

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:06.110
and as I have indicated, many millions are
kept out of poverty by its provisions.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:12.560
Its particular value is that it appeals to
the middle-income group, which is very largely

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:17.900
responsible for most of the things that happen
in the United States, in the political field,

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:24.289
while at the same time, receiving some support,
even enthusiastic support from high-income

00:07:24.289 --> 00:07:25.289
people.

00:07:25.289 --> 00:07:30.560
Very few programs in the United States can
say just exactly what I have said, that it

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.470
enjoys the support of all income classes very
substantially.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:41.590
But in addition, it enjoys the support of
blacks and other minority groups in our country

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:48.800
who believe that the payment of benefits as
a matter of statutory right without a means

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:55.650
test is one of the best protections to them
against discrimination and variation in benefits,

00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:03.259
which comes from attempting to make the payments
determined by either states, or localities,

00:08:03.259 --> 00:08:09.020
or income, or some other item that does not
give them the degree of security or assurance

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:11.370
which the present program does.

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:19.319
If success in my opinion is because it has
in it the principles that reinforce the capitalistic

00:08:19.319 --> 00:08:26.720
free enterprise system since it emphasizes
thrift and saving, since it provides for individual

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:33.120
responsibility in making the individual employee
and his employer contribute for it.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:39.330
And because its variable benefits in relation
to wages reinforces that element in our free

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:47.910
enterprise system, which varies wages and
varies rewards in relation to your contribution

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:51.860
to the system as expressed by the wage and
salary system.

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:57.870
Therefore, those who propose to change it
must in my opinion demonstrate that they have

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:03.740
a better system that will reinforce our free
enterprise system, that will win the support

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:08.860
of those who pay the taxes, and that they
will win the support of those who believe

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:14.670
that this system does give them a measure
of security.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:20.720
The basic element in the social security system
of course is that it does not have a means

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:30.610
test, a resource test, or a income related
test of any kind that is related to the point

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:36.149
where you have to show your income or your
resources.

00:09:36.149 --> 00:09:40.589
That is of course what is most meaningful
to the poor, and the middle class, and the

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:46.920
minority groups, and any substitution for
the present system which would provide an

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:53.149
income related test in its stead will of course
meet serious objections from the American

00:09:53.149 --> 00:10:00.000
public and therefore would have to be carefully
substantiated before a change could be made.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.050
I would add only this.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:09.620
The social security system is not simply an
economic institution.

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:16.160
Man does not live by bread alone, nor are
all of the elements of an institution solely

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.930
determined by economists.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:26.950
In the social security system is a mixture,
a rare mixture of political sagacity, of economics,

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:34.360
of compassion, of understanding, of institutionalizing
in our political system certain rights and

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:41.180
responsibilities, of income provided to people,
at the same time, tying closely a relationship

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:43.240
to paying the cost.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.620
That is a very, very important element in
the system and one which cannot simply be

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:56.000
analyzed strictly on the basis of income and
outgo by economists.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.709
Political figures, sociologists, those interested
in other aspects of our society have a role

00:11:01.709 --> 00:11:03.370
to play.

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:10.640
I am very happy that the Chicago School of
Millicent Douglas before 1932 played a big

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.149
role in creating this system.

00:11:13.149 --> 00:11:20.290
And along with those from Wisconsin and Princeton,
they provided the basic fabric of what is

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:26.180
one of the most remarkable and successful
programs that we have had in the United States.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:31.130
Of course can be improved and it should be
improved.

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:37.170
In addition, of course we must find a better
way to be able to deal with the problems of

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:42.300
poverty and the problems of income distribution
in our country.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:49.370
I have long publicly stated that I favor a
welfare system, a welfare reform, an income

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:54.899
payment program which is administered solely
by the federal government, which is administered

00:11:54.899 --> 00:11:59.280
and financed by the federal government out
of general revenues.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:04.010
But that system should not be a substitute
for social security.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:10.579
It should be a transitional system as best
we can make it, which would enable us along

00:12:10.579 --> 00:12:15.700
with other measures in the field of education,
in the fields of jobs, in the fields of public

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:21.910
service employment, and many other types of
programs to reduce the extent of poverty but

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:30.040
to make a social security system a benefit
payable as a matter of statutory right, the

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:36.050
basic system, and to have those improvements
over time made which will enable us both to

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:41.700
eliminate poverty, but at the same time to
have a program as social security does, which

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:49.000
reinforces thrift, which reinforces initiative,
which reinforces the program of rewards in

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.350
relation to productivity.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:58.920
And I believe that it provides a sound basis
for the improvements for the future.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:06.279
Peter Lisagor: Thank You Mr. Cohen.

00:13:06.279 --> 00:13:10.190
Astonishingly, Mr. Cohen stayed within his
allotted time.

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:14.800
I wasn't aware that there was a Chicago School
before Milton Friedman but now we know there

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:15.800
was.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:19.370
So I give you, Milton Friedman.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:27.810
Milton Friedman: If I seem a bit confused as
I talk to you, it's because I'm trying to

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:29.410
figure out where I am.

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:39.100
Literally, I ended up to the left of Mr. Lisagor,
which is no mean feat and to the right of

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:44.290
Alfred Landon which is no mean feat.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.949
Wilbur Cohen tells me I don't even belong
in Cook County.

00:13:47.949 --> 00:13:54.709
So, I will try to figure out where I am but
in the meantime, let me turn to the subject

00:13:54.709 --> 00:13:58.550
and my subject is really two separate programs.

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:04.170
The social security program of which Wilbur
Cohen spoke and also the welfare program of

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:06.230
which he spoke.

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:10.550
These two programs were both introduced in
the Great Depression.

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:19.130
Supposedly, the first as a permanent program
to enable people to have benefits as a matter

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:24.240
of right, and the second, the public assistance
program is a temporary program for people

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:25.990
in distress.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:30.910
Like most government programs of course, both
are still with us and larger than they ever

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:31.910
were.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:36.260
I want to talk first a little bit about social
security, then about welfare, and then try

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:38.040
to put the two together.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:43.550
The great problem as I said in discussing
social security, and for simplicity, I am

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:47.670
restricting my comments to old-age and survivors
insurance.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:54.820
The great problem in discussing that program
is to separate rhetoric from reality.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:59.589
Mr. Cohen gave you the rhetoric, so I will
try to give you the reality.

00:14:59.589 --> 00:15:06.589
The rhetoric is that the social security program
is an insurance program in which individuals

00:15:06.589 --> 00:15:13.210
have benefits as a matter of right assured
to them by the accumulation of a trust fund.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:15.600
This is pure rhetoric.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:22.750
The reality is that any relationship between
social security and private insurance in which

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:28.510
individual benefits, individual payments by
individual benefits is purely coincidental.

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:36.250
The benefits that an individual may receive
may vary widely even though he pays the same

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:38.380
amount of taxes as somebody else.

00:15:38.380 --> 00:15:45.670
The taxes people pay very enormously widely
even though they receive the same benefits.

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:52.009
In fact, the benefits which are related to
earnings vary over a range of three or four

00:15:52.009 --> 00:15:53.620
to one.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:59.250
The taxes that people may pay to acquire those
vary over a range of something like 80 to

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.440
1.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:05.100
Millions of people who pay taxes will never
receive any benefits because they haven't...attributable

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:10.839
to those taxes because they haven't paid for
long enough or because they receive benefits

00:16:10.839 --> 00:16:14.310
as spouse instead of in their own right.

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:20.630
The reality is that any assurance people have
of benefits does not derive from a trust fund.

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.329
There is a trust fund.

00:16:22.329 --> 00:16:26.060
It's roughly of a little over 30...between
$30 billion and $35 billion.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:31.370
Some 10 years ago, Mr. Myers who was here
from the social security board estimated that

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:36.730
it would take a $300 billion trust fund to
meet the obligations then outstanding.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:39.630
Today, that figure would be vastly higher.

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:47.600
The truth is that if you strip the rhetoric
from the reality, the truth is...and what

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.540
you have is a system under which there's a
tax on the one hand and there are benefits

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:52.550
on the other.

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:59.389
The taxes that you and I pay are being used
to pay benefits to the people who today are

00:16:59.389 --> 00:17:04.130
receiving their pensions.

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:09.079
That may be a good thing, it may be a bad
thing, but it is a very different thing from

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:14.919
individual benefits actually related to individual
payments.

00:17:14.919 --> 00:17:19.370
What assurance do people have that they will
get their benefits in the future?

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:26.500
The reality is that the only assurance they
have is confidence that future taxpayers will

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:31.360
validate the promises that present taxpayers
are making.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:35.710
You and I are promising ourselves benefits.

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:41.830
The assurance that they will be paid is that
future taxpayers, our children, will in their

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:46.549
turn impose taxes on themselves sufficient
to pay those benefits.

00:17:46.549 --> 00:17:53.010
That may be a sufficient assurance but it's
a very different thing from a trust fund.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:59.299
I think the right way to look at social security
is as a tax on the one hand and a benefit

00:17:59.299 --> 00:18:00.780
structure on the other.

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:04.919
And there the thing that has impressed me
over and over again is that I know hardly

00:18:04.919 --> 00:18:12.130
anybody who will defend the tax system by
itself, who will defend a regressive tax,

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:16.260
a payroll tax on income up to a maximum.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:21.540
I know hardly anybody who will defend the
benefit system by itself, who will defend

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:26.490
the benefit system in which some people in
great need get nothing, other people who have

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:31.660
high incomes get the maximum amount that the
system offers.

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:33.480
And there are many more detailed versions.

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:39.299
And yet when you put the two together, the
two together have become a sacred cow that

00:18:39.299 --> 00:18:44.410
Mr. Wilbur Cohen quite properly described
as widely approved and widely backed.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:49.550
I think this is one of the most extraordinary
cases of imaginative back packaging that the

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:51.980
commercial world has ever seen.

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:55.800
Now, let me turn for a moment to the welfare
system.

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:58.790
Oh, I want to go back and say one more thing.

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:03.539
If you look at the combined tax and benefit
system, I believe there is little doubt that

00:19:03.539 --> 00:19:10.080
on balance, it provides benefits to people
in higher income classes than on the average

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.010
the people who pay the taxes.

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:16.460
That's not true in every individual case but
if you examine the system as I've tried to

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:20.830
in my fuller paper, as it would operate on
a full basis, I believe there is a strong

00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:27.140
presumption that the old-age and survivors
insurance system transfers income from low-income

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:29.270
groups to middle and upper income groups.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:32.020
Now, I want to turn to welfare.

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:37.750
The welfare system I can treat more readily
because we all agree it's a mess.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:42.090
It's a system which is highly inequitable,
it's a system which has reduced incentives

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:47.600
to work, it is a system which has demeaned
many people by separating people into two

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:53.130
classes, welfare recipients and others, and
inducing welfare recipients to remain in that

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.830
category on a permanent basis.

00:19:56.830 --> 00:20:01.770
I think that system should be reformed, and
the direction in which it should be reformed

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:06.850
is the direction which President Nixon has
proposed in the Family Assistance Plan, a

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:12.260
negative income tax, although I don't agree
with the particular structure of that plan,

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:14.169
it doesn't provide enough incentive.

00:20:14.169 --> 00:20:19.500
Now, what shall we do about these two programs
taken together?

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:24.410
In my view, we should not have two separate
programs.

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:30.920
In my view, the government has no business
doing for people what people can do for themselves.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:35.890
Individuals separately are perfectly capable
of providing for their own retirement.

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:41.020
What I would like my government to do, what
I would like to do as a taxpayer in my government

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:48.111
is to contribute to a program which assures
people in dire distress of a minimum assistance

00:20:48.111 --> 00:20:52.980
to keep them from destitution regardless of
why they are destitute.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:58.590
Whether they are old, whether they are unemployed,
whether they are disabled, whatever may be

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:03.799
the reason why they are destitute, I would
like to us to have a single program which

00:21:03.799 --> 00:21:10.720
would provide assistance and a minimum scale
to enable them to avoid the destitution.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:17.460
And therefore, I believe that the right road
for the future is to underline social security,

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:19.809
meet every promise we have so far made.

00:21:19.809 --> 00:21:24.140
We have made promises and commitments and
we should fulfill every one.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:30.000
But underline social security on the one hand,
and on the other, reform our welfare program

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:36.580
in such a way that it will provide assistance
to all who need it regardless of the reason

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:38.210
for their need.

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:42.720
The details of how to do that I have tried
to spell out a little in the larger paper

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:44.500
that has been available to you.

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:50.580
I cannot go into it here except to say that
on the social security side, the idea would

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:59.860
be to provide people with firm vested commitments
for whatever benefits they have so far earned

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:05.980
under the social security program, and then
eliminate the payroll tax and eliminate the

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:08.330
further accumulation of rights.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:14.799
On the welfare side, the idea would be to
have a full-fledged negative income tax with

00:22:14.799 --> 00:22:19.659
a marginal tax rate that would preserve a
very large measure of incentive.

00:22:19.659 --> 00:22:21.470
I would hope a rate of not more than 50%.

00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:28.120
Now, let me end by reading the final three
paragraphs of the paper I submitted because

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:31.960
I believe it puts in a nutshell what I want
to say.

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:38.110
Social security programs involve annual expenditures
of over $43 billion.

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:44.220
These programs appear on balance to transfer
income from relatively low income classes

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:46.340
to middle and upper income classes.

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:51.590
They are flourishing, are widely regarded
as highly successful, and seem destined to

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:53.539
expand.

00:22:53.539 --> 00:22:58.230
Welfare programs involve annual expenditures
of $15 billion.

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:04.559
These programs appear to on balance to transfer
income from middle and upper income classes

00:23:04.559 --> 00:23:05.860
to lower income classes.

00:23:05.860 --> 00:23:11.970
They are in a state of crisis, are widely
regarded as intolerable, and seem destined

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:14.059
to be transformed.

00:23:14.059 --> 00:23:19.490
Is this another example of what George Stigler
has called Directors law in operation.

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:24.070
I read you Stigler's formulation of that law.

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:30.750
"Public expenditures are made for the primary
benefit of the middle class and financed with

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:37.250
taxes that are borne in considerable part
by the poor and the rich."

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:40.850
Thank you.

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:45.830
Mr. Lisagor: Thank You Professor Friedman.

00:23:45.830 --> 00:23:50.430
Now, Mr. Cohen will have five minutes for
rebuttal.

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:57.669
Wilbur Cohen: Professor Friedman makes a good
point that when you look at the social security

00:23:57.669 --> 00:24:05.190
system alone as a tax or as a benefit, it
probably would not be justified as separately.

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:10.650
But that does not make the argument sound
in saying that when you look at them together,

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:13.760
they are not sound.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:20.080
My analogy would be a man alone can be looked
at alone, a woman alone can be looked at alone,

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:23.830
but when you put them together, something
happens.

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:26.440
That's what's happened in social security.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:35.680
Putting them together has been the art of
doing something and it's more than rhetoric.

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:42.659
Professor Friedman also in a sense of tax,
the concept of insurance, and that is mainly

00:24:42.659 --> 00:24:46.200
because he doesn't know what the concept of
insurance is.

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:53.380
He defines it in a Friedman definition but
not the right definition.

00:24:53.380 --> 00:25:00.330
Insurance does not mean that you have to have
an actuarial relationship between the individual

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:03.000
contributions and the individual benefits.

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:10.020
If so, all group insurance says in the United
States would not be insurance under his test.

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:15.130
I won't argue about his definition, if he
wants to have his definition, let him have

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:16.130
it.

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:20.150
It's not my definition, it's not the definition
that the Congress of the United States has

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:21.150
given.

00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:22.400
They've called it insurance.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:27.310
The Supreme Court of the United States has
called it insurance, and my article in the

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:32.779
Encyclopedia Britannica calls it insurance.

00:25:32.779 --> 00:25:38.789
Take your pick.

00:25:38.789 --> 00:25:43.380
Professor Friedman calls a lot of the things
that he doesn't like about social security,

00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:46.140
rhetoric, and that's exactly my point.

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:53.590
My point is the economists of this country
do not determine all of the choices, and options,

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:57.179
and attitudes, and things that go on in this
nation.

00:25:57.179 --> 00:25:58.910
People do live by rhetoric.

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:02.970
You couldn't understand what goes on in the
United States if you didn't understand something

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:04.179
about rhetoric.

00:26:04.179 --> 00:26:08.260
And think of all the people in this audience
that we put out of a job if we didn't have

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:11.860
such a thing as rhetoric.

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:16.659
I believe in rhetoric because it makes a lot
of things palatable that might be unpalatable

00:26:16.659 --> 00:26:20.380
to economists.

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:28.929
Finally, he attacks the idea, to some extent
rightfully, that the present system is a system

00:26:28.929 --> 00:26:33.120
primarily of redistribution of income to middle-income
people.

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:39.210
I think he's actually right and that is the
political sagacity in the social security

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:40.390
system.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:45.549
Since most of the people in the United States
are in the middle-income middle class system,

00:26:45.549 --> 00:26:47.580
it is a program which appeals to them.

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:53.039
Now, to the extent that he makes a point that
there is a transfer of income from low income

00:26:53.039 --> 00:26:57.679
people to middle income that could easily
be rectified by certain changes in the financing.

00:26:57.679 --> 00:27:01.789
You don't have to do away with the social
security system to rectify that.

00:27:01.789 --> 00:27:07.029
But the reason why, oh we oh, and all other
types of programs that don't appeal to the

00:27:07.029 --> 00:27:11.690
middle class, don't get appropriations, don't
get support from the taxpayers, is simply

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:15.970
because they do not appeal to the middle class,
middle-income person.

00:27:15.970 --> 00:27:21.169
True, if you're an economist you may exclude
all matters of politics, but I would then

00:27:21.169 --> 00:27:25.039
say that's not reality, Mr. Milton.

00:27:25.039 --> 00:27:29.880
And so, I believe that the essence of social
security in appealing to middle income is

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:35.210
desirable and those things that are criticized
in social security could easily be remedied

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:36.850
by certain changes.

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:46.350
Now, my major objection to the negative income
tax as a complete substitute for social security

00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:51.030
is that I am convinced in the present situation
in the United States, a program that only

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:57.830
deals with the poor will end up being a poor
program, and there is every evidence on my

00:27:57.830 --> 00:28:00.600
side that that is true.

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:07.490
Programs only for the poor, since 1601 under
the Elizabethan Poor Law have been lousy,

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:10.659
no-good, poor programs.

00:28:10.659 --> 00:28:15.490
And having a program that is only for the
poor, not for the middle-income, not for the

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:20.669
upper income, is a program that will not in
the long time be financed and supported by

00:28:20.669 --> 00:28:21.860
the American public.

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:28.620
And for that reason I think one must try to
find a way to join this program.

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:34.090
I would therefore argue that a continuation
of the social security system with some support

00:28:34.090 --> 00:28:39.771
underneath of a guaranteed income, negative
income tax, welfare reform, national welfare

00:28:39.771 --> 00:28:46.490
system is more in keeping with the American
system of free enterprise and with our political

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:53.960
system than dumping social security and substituting
for it therefore a poor program only for poor

00:28:53.960 --> 00:28:58.860
people, which in the end would not give security
to poor people either.

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:07.940
Peter Lisagor: Thank You Mr. Cohen, and now
for a five-minute rebuttal by Professor Friedman.

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:13.600
Milton Friedman: As many of you probably know,
there are quite a number of state legislators

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:17.840
that at one time or another have passed a
law saying that the value of pi should be

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:18.940
three.

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:27.789
According to Mr. Cohen's analysis of the meaning
of insurance, that means that pi is now three.

00:29:27.789 --> 00:29:34.659
I recognize of course that insurance does
not mean that individual payments are equal

00:29:34.659 --> 00:29:40.380
to individual benefits but rather that individual
payments have an actuarial equivalent in the

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:42.309
benefits that are associated with them.

00:29:42.309 --> 00:29:47.520
And there is no, I assure you, and in that
interpretation of insurance which is the interpretation

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:53.930
that is widely accepted in you, there is very,
very little connection between social security

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:58.830
as it is now set up in any kind of an insurance
program you can dream of.

00:29:58.830 --> 00:30:03.740
Now, of course combining the taxes and the
benefits does serve a purpose.

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:06.570
It serves a political purpose.

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:12.490
It serves a political purpose of getting people
to accept something under false pretenses.

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:16.860
It serves a purpose of selling to people a
program which they would not buy if they knew

00:30:16.860 --> 00:30:18.380
what they were buying.

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:23.920
This is a political role of linking them together
and this is what I meant when I said, it was

00:30:23.920 --> 00:30:27.750
a triumph of imaginative packaging.

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:33.409
But as an economic matter, as a political
matter, other than getting it adopted, the

00:30:33.409 --> 00:30:39.330
combining of the two halves is not, I'm sorry
to say, anything like the ineffable chemistry

00:30:39.330 --> 00:30:48.179
of combining man and woman.

00:30:48.179 --> 00:30:56.530
I am really a little puzzled by the argument
Mr. Cohen makes here and in his paper that

00:30:56.530 --> 00:31:01.590
hinges around the phrase a program for the
poor will be a for poor program that hinges

00:31:01.590 --> 00:31:10.110
around the statement that he recognizes that
the social security program provides disproportionate

00:31:10.110 --> 00:31:14.409
benefits to middle classes but that's the
price of getting it enacted.

00:31:14.409 --> 00:31:16.659
And what puzzles me is this.

00:31:16.659 --> 00:31:22.049
Are we really helping poor people by saying
to them, "Here is a program under which we

00:31:22.049 --> 00:31:29.470
will take $2 from you and give you $1 back
and we can get that program passed, but we

00:31:29.470 --> 00:31:33.430
can't get a program passed which will take
$1 from you and give you a $1 back."

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:40.980
Well, if that's the case, it seems to me that
if I were one of those poor people, if I were

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:45.090
one of the people who Mr. Cohen is talking
to and saying, "Don't worry, we'll take care

00:31:45.090 --> 00:31:46.090
of you.

00:31:46.090 --> 00:31:49.860
We promise that for every $2 we take from
you, you'll get one back."

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:53.659
I would repeat Thoreau's wonderful comment
that when I hear that people are coming to

00:31:53.659 --> 00:31:57.890
do me good, I run as fast as I can to get
away from them.

00:31:57.890 --> 00:32:02.910
Now, I think fundamentally what is at bottom
of the difference between Mr. Cohen and me,

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:08.670
and here I agree with him is not economics,
is not economic analysis, but it's basic philosophy.

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:12.540
I believe there is a fundamental difference
between two philosophies.

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:17.130
One philosophy is that of a society in which
the ultimate entity is the individual, in

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:23.510
which the society rests upon the idea that
people are responsible for themselves and

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:26.260
that people are responsible for those close
to them.

00:32:26.260 --> 00:32:33.100
That if I have a moral responsibility, it
is an individual responsibility, then I cannot

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:37.440
discharge by paying taxes levied on me by
some Congress.

00:32:37.440 --> 00:32:39.990
I have to discharge it personally.

00:32:39.990 --> 00:32:42.190
That's one view, that's my view.

00:32:42.190 --> 00:32:48.350
I believe that Mr. Cohen's view, one which
unfortunately is very widespread and very

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:50.170
widely held is different.

00:32:50.170 --> 00:32:55.860
It treats the nation as not a collection of
individuals and of the groups which individuals

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:59.940
separately value but as an organismic unit.

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:03.409
I read from his paper.

00:33:03.409 --> 00:33:08.600
Says Mr. Cohen, "I speak for the view that
the nation should be generous toward the elderly."

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:11.920
I would argue that the nation can't be generous
to anything.

00:33:11.920 --> 00:33:14.140
Only people can be generous.

00:33:14.140 --> 00:33:17.899
Generosity is a human individual trait, not
a collective trait.

00:33:17.899 --> 00:33:27.000
There's no generosity involved in my imposing
taxes on you to help him, that's not generosity.

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:33.640
But nonetheless, underlying the social security
view, and it is a view that is very widespread.

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:40.530
Is that society is an organism, that there
is a group within the society who knows what's

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:46.890
good for other people better than they do
themselves, and that that elite has an obligation,

00:33:46.890 --> 00:33:51.950
a duty, and a responsibility to induce the
public at large to pass the laws that will

00:33:51.950 --> 00:33:53.480
be good for them.

00:33:53.480 --> 00:33:57.980
That in particular, it has the obligation
to package this program in a way in which

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:01.980
they will adopt it even if they would not
adopt each part separately.

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:07.200
Now, obviously that's not the way Mr. Cohen
would describe his philosophy but he is an

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:10.190
eloquent man and describes it very well for
himself.

00:34:10.190 --> 00:34:14.639
But it does seem to me that fundamentally
at bottom, you do have these different views,

00:34:14.639 --> 00:34:21.639
and that he is right in saying that there
is a far more fundamental issue at stake than

00:34:21.639 --> 00:34:25.829
the issue, the technical issues involved in
the detail of the program.

00:34:25.829 --> 00:34:31.169
That fundamental issue is as we look toward
the future, do we want to see our society

00:34:31.169 --> 00:34:36.710
develop in a way in which individuals separately
will have greater responsibilities or do we

00:34:36.710 --> 00:34:43.030
want to see it develop in a way in which the
collective entity is more dominant and more

00:34:43.030 --> 00:34:44.730
omnipresent?

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:50.359
Speaking for myself, I would say that our
growing wealth, our growing income makes it

00:34:50.359 --> 00:34:56.450
all the more possible for us to let individuals
separately run their own lives.

00:34:56.450 --> 00:35:00.270
And I would like to see us develop in a way
which gives greater freedom to each one of

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:05.220
us separately and which reduces the role of
collective entity.

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:08.599
Thank you.

00:35:08.599 --> 00:35:15.810
Peter: They are cogent arguments as we've
seen on both sides of this issue of social

00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:17.150
security.

00:35:17.150 --> 00:35:21.880
Dean Cohen feels the social security system
is a sound one, that its shortcomings can

00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:26.750
be cured by changing the present system rather
than by adopting a new one.

00:35:26.750 --> 00:35:33.310
Dr. Friedman to make use of his own words
wants to reform welfare and unwind social

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:34.900
security.

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:40.510
We go now to our panel of experts for their
questions and for answers from our speakers.

00:35:40.510 --> 00:35:48.849
Mr Lisagor: Would you kindly identify yourselves
and direct your questions at one or both of

00:35:48.849 --> 00:35:49.849
the speakers.

00:35:49.849 --> 00:35:51.000
Who has the first question, yes?

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.530
Dorothy: I'm Dorothy McClellan, Senate Committee
on aging.

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:57.020
I have a question for Mr. Friedman.

00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:08.180
With his last estimate from Bob Myers of $300
billion deferred cost.

00:36:08.180 --> 00:36:14.890
What happens to the economy if the nation's
workers are faced with the collapse of a system

00:36:14.890 --> 00:36:16.339
in which they believe?

00:36:16.339 --> 00:36:20.900
How do they get their $300 billion out right
now?

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:27.940
What happens in the future to provide any
incentive for people to save through private

00:36:27.940 --> 00:36:35.030
pensions or through individual savings when
whatever income they managed to put by is

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:38.580
going to make them ineligible for a needs
test payment.

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:43.990
Milton Friedman: Well, you've asked a number
of questions together.

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:46.109
Let me take them one at a time.

00:36:46.109 --> 00:36:50.839
First, with respect to the $300 billion, we
have that in which probably is $500 billion

00:36:50.839 --> 00:36:54.480
or something like that now, we have that obligation
now.

00:36:54.480 --> 00:37:01.150
The only point is that it is not funded, it
is not written on paper, no economic fact

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:04.180
has changed by funding it.

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:07.180
It's got to be paid now somehow or other in
the future.

00:37:07.180 --> 00:37:11.359
And what I'm proposing here is that we recognize
that we have that obligation, and we write

00:37:11.359 --> 00:37:16.220
it down on pieces of paper, that we fund it
in the form of an explicit government debt

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:20.650
instead of an implicit one, and in the details
which I have in the paper.

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:25.690
I have funding it in a way in which workers
would get it ultimately at the same time as

00:37:25.690 --> 00:37:32.460
they now would but I want to enable us to
discontinue further regressive taxation of

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:33.680
the workers.

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:36.510
So, there will be no collapse of the system.

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:41.960
That's just again a figment of the imagination,
the fact is you now have that debt, you now

00:37:41.960 --> 00:37:43.380
have that obligation.

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:46.650
If that's going to collapse the system, the
system should collapse now.

00:37:46.650 --> 00:37:48.990
Second, the question of incentive.

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:52.010
The facts are almost the reverse of what you're
speaking of.

00:37:52.010 --> 00:37:56.349
Our present social security tax is a large
disincentive.

00:37:56.349 --> 00:38:03.520
Anybody who goes to work, over 10% of his
wage cost at the low level up to $7800 now,

00:38:03.520 --> 00:38:06.079
it will be higher later on.

00:38:06.079 --> 00:38:12.510
Over 10% of his wage cost to his employer
is now taken out and sent to social security

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:15.300
and he only gets less than 90% of it.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:18.710
Therefore, he does not have sufficient incentive
now.

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:23.369
Indeed, one of the great problems of providing
sufficient incentive for people to get off

00:38:23.369 --> 00:38:29.040
welfare is that if they get off welfare and
take a job, well, then over and above, any

00:38:29.040 --> 00:38:34.480
reduction in their welfare benefits they now
have to start paying social security taxes,

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:42.079
they have to pay 10.8% of their wage cost
in taxes, and that raises a marginal rate.

00:38:42.079 --> 00:38:45.050
In fact, I was astounded when I went through
these numbers.

00:38:45.050 --> 00:38:51.330
I had always thought the disincentive to work
was associated with a high tax rate of the

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:52.859
personal income tax.

00:38:52.859 --> 00:38:59.250
I am now persuaded that the social security
tax is a greater disincentive to work, that

00:38:59.250 --> 00:39:03.850
are the high rates of the personal income
tax because it hits people lower down, it

00:39:03.850 --> 00:39:08.109
hits people who have the alternative of relief,
and it hits them hard.

00:39:08.109 --> 00:39:14.080
The social security tax paid on behalf of
people today is for most people decidedly

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:16.070
higher than their personal income tax.

00:39:16.070 --> 00:39:22.640
So in my belief, in my opinion, eliminating
the payroll tax would add to incentives of

00:39:22.640 --> 00:39:26.320
people to work, it would give us a larger
output, it would make it more feasible for

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:34.040
people to put their own funds aside, to provide
for their later benefits, for their own private pensions.

00:39:36.380 --> 00:39:41.120
Wilbur Cohen: First, I don't think there's any
really empirical evidence that Friedman can

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:45.660
support the show that the payroll tax has
been a disincentive.

00:39:45.660 --> 00:39:52.930
Number two, if social security were abolished
and left to private enterprise, then the unions

00:39:52.930 --> 00:39:58.359
through collective bargaining would see that
the same tax would be put on through collective

00:39:58.359 --> 00:39:59.359
bargaining.

00:39:59.359 --> 00:40:03.230
So the net economic effect that he's suggesting
is exactly the same.

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:06.960
If they didn't put it on through collective
bargaining, it would then have to be paid

00:40:06.960 --> 00:40:12.060
out of general revenues in which the only
way you get the money is raising the marginal

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:15.790
rates back from 70% to 90% as they were before.

00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:21.190
Now, what I'd like to know from Professor
Friedman, which of those two does he recommend?

00:40:21.190 --> 00:40:25.880
The union's bargaining with the employers
to put it on through payroll taxes or raising

00:40:25.880 --> 00:40:27.570
the marginal rates back to 90%?

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:28.570
Milton Friedman: Neither.

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:31.300
Wilbur Cohen: How are you gonna get the money
then?

00:40:31.300 --> 00:40:32.609
Milton Friedman: Well, the money for which?

00:40:32.609 --> 00:40:34.390
For paying the debt we now have in the United...

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:35.420
Wilbur Cohen: No, no, no.

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:38.120
For giving people the security which they
would get through the private enterprises

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:39.120
system.

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:44.240
Milton Friedman: In the first place, there is
an enormous difference between individuals

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:50.030
separately paying over part of their wage
to buy a pension which they separately desire.

00:40:50.030 --> 00:40:51.300
That is not a disincentive.

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:57.480
If I spent some of my income to buy a pension
out of my own free will, that's part of my

00:40:57.480 --> 00:40:59.460
payment, that is not a disincentive.

00:40:59.460 --> 00:41:05.589
The disincentive arises because the social
security tax which is deducted from my salary

00:41:05.589 --> 00:41:11.300
has a very minor relation to any benefit which
I will subsequently get.

00:41:11.300 --> 00:41:14.410
Wilbur Cohen: But you're living in an unreal
world Professor Friedman.

00:41:14.410 --> 00:41:19.020
You know that if the social security were
repealed, it wouldn't be left to individual

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:24.920
John Jones, the unions would bargain with
the employers to put that cost right back

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:31.450
entirely on the employer, which would have
exactly the same economic effect as it has

00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:32.450
now.

00:41:32.450 --> 00:41:35.319
You'd have larger reserves than you had now.

00:41:35.319 --> 00:41:37.359
So what would you have gained economically?

00:41:37.359 --> 00:41:38.840
You'd have the same disincentive.

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:41.490
Milton Friedman: What you're saying is wrong.

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:42.859
It is wrong for several reasons.

00:41:42.859 --> 00:41:49.180
It is wrong first because only 25% of workers
are today unionized and most workers are not.

00:41:49.180 --> 00:41:54.460
It is wrong in the second place because the
unions would only bargain for such pension

00:41:54.460 --> 00:41:59.210
benefits of this kind as the workers would
prefer, their union members would prefer,

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:04.740
the union leaders aren't after all the employees,
the agents of their own members, they would

00:42:04.740 --> 00:42:11.410
only bargain for such benefits as their own
members would prefer to have in the form of

00:42:11.410 --> 00:42:14.099
pensions rather than in the form of cash.

00:42:14.099 --> 00:42:19.349
In the third place, of course both under social
security and under union arrangements, there

00:42:19.349 --> 00:42:24.510
is no important difference between whether
in the first instance attack, the sum is paid

00:42:24.510 --> 00:42:25.920
by the employer or the employee.

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:29.250
Wilbur Cohen: I agree! Therefore the unions and others would put the whole cost on the employer.

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:33.850
Milton Friedman: No, no, I have to stop because
this is a historic moment.

00:42:33.850 --> 00:42:39.450
Mr. Cohen and every single thing I have ever
seen that he and other people have written

00:42:39.450 --> 00:42:42.550
has denied the proposition he just agreed
to.

00:42:42.550 --> 00:42:43.550
He agreed now that the....

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:45.950
Wilbur Cohen: I have never denied it, I have
never denied it.

00:42:45.950 --> 00:42:49.270
You've never seen or heard where I've denied
it.

00:42:49.270 --> 00:42:56.170
I justify it not on economics, I support it
on the basis of aesthetic logic, which you

00:42:56.170 --> 00:42:57.170
don't recognize.

00:42:57.170 --> 00:43:03.380
Milton Friedman: Excuse me, I have seen you write,
I have seen things written under your name

00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:06.050
maybe you didn't write them but your name
was signed on it.

00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:12.760
I have seen certain things written under your
name in which you compared the benefits received

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:21.680
by an individual to the employee tax without
making mention of the employer tax and implying

00:43:21.680 --> 00:43:26.540
that that employer tax could be neglected
in relating individual payments to individual

00:43:26.540 --> 00:43:27.540
benefit.

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:33.369
Wilbur Cohen: What you have seen but not correctly
interpreted is the fact that you use the employee

00:43:33.369 --> 00:43:40.050
contribution as a measure of equity in the
system but there is also such a thing as social

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:41.200
adequacy.

00:43:41.200 --> 00:43:47.460
If you read Reinhard Hohaus, the vice-president
Metropolitan's famous essay of 1937 on equity

00:43:47.460 --> 00:43:52.630
and social adequacy in social insurance, you'll
see that both elements are given some weight.

00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:57.880
I completely support the idea that in social
insurance, you have two attributes.

00:43:57.880 --> 00:44:00.650
An equity concept and a social concept.

00:44:00.650 --> 00:44:04.930
That is the ark that's made social security
a acceptable system.

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:08.319
Milton Friedman: We are talking across purposes,
I want to ask you a simpler question, a very

00:44:08.319 --> 00:44:09.319
simple question.

00:44:09.319 --> 00:44:15.359
Do you agree that the tax on the employer,
that the division of the total tax between

00:44:15.359 --> 00:44:22.260
the tax on the employer and the tax on the
employee has little or no effect on who pays

00:44:22.260 --> 00:44:23.260
it?

00:44:23.260 --> 00:44:24.260
Wilbur Cohen: That is correct, so what?

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:26.829
So what do you draw from that?

00:44:26.829 --> 00:44:27.829
Milton Friedman: If that is correct...

00:44:27.829 --> 00:44:28.829
I draw.

00:44:28.829 --> 00:44:31.330
Man: Excuse me I think I'll just go.

00:44:31.330 --> 00:44:37.180
Milton Friedman: Well, as I say, I regard that
as a historic moment because I have never

00:44:37.180 --> 00:44:42.440
seen in any social security document or in
any document written by you that admission.

00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:47.260
And that admission means that many of the
statements made by you and by social security

00:44:47.260 --> 00:44:53.380
about the relation of benefits to amounts
paid or to put it, to not to put a fine word

00:44:53.380 --> 00:44:54.380
on it, are pure hogwash.

00:44:54.380 --> 00:44:59.050
Mr. Cohen: Well, you know, there's an old
Yiddish term called chutzpah.

00:44:59.050 --> 00:45:03.760
Milton Friedman: And you and I both have our
share.

00:45:03.760 --> 00:45:09.470
Mr. Cohen: Professor Friedman demonstrates
chutzpah better than anyone else I know.

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:15.349
When he disagrees with anything that somebody
has said he says you're not meeting my definition.

00:45:15.349 --> 00:45:23.190
Well, the point is simply that the social
security system does meet these tests of insurance,

00:45:23.190 --> 00:45:27.940
meets a test of equity, meets a test of social
adequacy, and the fact that he doesn't agree

00:45:27.940 --> 00:45:33.819
with it, you know, he's not Congress, he's
not the American people, he's only an economist.

00:45:33.819 --> 00:45:36.800
Milton Friedman: Thank God for small favors.

00:45:36.800 --> 00:45:45.440
Peter Lisagor: Should we try another question?

00:45:45.440 --> 00:45:49.250
Theodore: I'm Theodore Shorkit
of North American Newspaper Alliance.

00:45:49.250 --> 00:45:53.330
My question is directed to Professor Friedman.

00:45:53.330 --> 00:46:01.390
In your paper and in your remarks, you've
stated that public assistance was intended

00:46:01.390 --> 00:46:05.980
as a temporary program for persons in distress
to be largely phased out as employment improved

00:46:05.980 --> 00:46:08.940
and as social security matured.

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:16.420
And then you say, there's no sign that social
security is displaced assistance, both were

00:46:16.420 --> 00:46:18.450
at all-time highs, etc.

00:46:18.450 --> 00:46:22.760
Now, aren't you mixing apples and oranges
here Professor Friedman?

00:46:22.760 --> 00:46:29.020
Isn't it a fact that the portion of the public
assistance program which is directed to the

00:46:29.020 --> 00:46:35.750
hazards at which the social security program
is directed, that is old-age and orphan, premature

00:46:35.750 --> 00:46:40.849
death of the breadwinners, isn't it a fact
that those portions of the public assistance

00:46:40.849 --> 00:46:46.440
roles have declined markedly, and to that
extent, the social security system has accomplished

00:46:46.440 --> 00:46:47.440
what it set out to do?

00:46:47.440 --> 00:46:54.460
The social security system was not created
and nor has it ever been in business to take

00:46:54.460 --> 00:46:58.220
care of family break up due to divorce and
desertion.

00:46:58.220 --> 00:47:04.089
Those are the factors which have made the
public assistance roles leap to an all-time

00:47:04.089 --> 00:47:05.089
high.

00:47:05.089 --> 00:47:12.300
Milton Friedman: There is no doubt that some
of the people who are receiving benefits under

00:47:12.300 --> 00:47:17.300
social security are people who in the absence
of social security, might be on the assistance

00:47:17.300 --> 00:47:19.100
roles, that's quite right.

00:47:19.100 --> 00:47:26.300
Now, the question...and I wouldn't want to
argue for a moment that there isn't some substitution

00:47:26.300 --> 00:47:28.099
or competition between the two.

00:47:28.099 --> 00:47:32.510
What I meant to say here is that in this initial
version, at large when I was trying to find

00:47:32.510 --> 00:47:38.860
a phrase in Mr. Cohen's paper which said essentially
the same thing about the initial intent.

00:47:38.860 --> 00:47:48.839
In the initial version, the idea was that
the...

00:47:48.839 --> 00:47:49.960
Here we are.

00:47:49.960 --> 00:47:56.420
Said Mr. Cohen, bottom of page 10, "The 1935
decision was to accept plan two with the hope

00:47:56.420 --> 00:47:59.109
that the welfare portion would ultimately
wither away."

00:47:59.109 --> 00:48:05.859
Now, the initial idea was that old age or
unemployment, the identifiable things were

00:48:05.859 --> 00:48:11.480
the major source of need for public assistance,
and that if those were adequately taken care

00:48:11.480 --> 00:48:17.210
of, the need for public assistance would largely
wither away and disappear.

00:48:17.210 --> 00:48:21.580
And all I was trying to say here is that that
has not been achieved, that on the contrary,

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:22.670
public assistance has grown.

00:48:22.670 --> 00:48:26.590
It might be that it would have grown still
more rapidly if there had been no social security

00:48:26.590 --> 00:48:29.329
system but that's by no means obvious.

00:48:29.329 --> 00:48:34.070
The reason it's by no means obvious is because
while social security has...while there are

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:38.830
some people now receiving benefits under social
security who would be under public assistance,

00:48:38.830 --> 00:48:42.829
there may well be some people under public
assistance who would not have been there if

00:48:42.829 --> 00:48:47.240
there had been no social security program
because you must look at the taxes that were

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:50.750
extracted from people as well as the benefits
they received.

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:57.110
Now, up to now the persons who have retired
have received much more than they ever paid

00:48:57.110 --> 00:48:58.140
separately as taxes.

00:48:58.140 --> 00:49:01.010
That's the difference between the young and
the old.

00:49:01.010 --> 00:49:07.650
And so undoubtedly today, more people are
displaced by social security than are added

00:49:07.650 --> 00:49:09.890
to the public assistance role by social security.

00:49:09.890 --> 00:49:12.020
But it's not obvious that's going to continue
to be the case.

00:49:12.020 --> 00:49:13.790
Mr. Lisagor: You still have your question?

00:49:13.790 --> 00:49:16.910
Bill: I've got a question for Mr. Cohen.

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:20.930
Bill Carlson, House Committee on Government
Operations.

00:49:20.930 --> 00:49:29.810
You place considerable emphasis on the public
acceptance of a social security system, do

00:49:29.810 --> 00:49:38.230
you see this continuing after the first enthusiastic
recipients, those who I think probably got

00:49:38.230 --> 00:49:42.359
a better bargain out of social security than
future recipients will get?

00:49:42.359 --> 00:49:47.030
Do you see this same public acceptance continuing?

00:49:47.030 --> 00:49:55.349
Mr. Cohen: I believe that as the payroll taxes
go up, there will be a greater degree of resistance

00:49:55.349 --> 00:50:02.640
against future increases beyond some limit
that people think is acceptable, and therefore

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:07.181
I believe that you will have to consider several
alternatives which I have written about in

00:50:07.181 --> 00:50:08.980
my public papers.

00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:11.740
And some of them may be as follows.

00:50:11.740 --> 00:50:17.440
Increasing the payroll tax on the employers
substantially above the present limit, providing

00:50:17.440 --> 00:50:23.880
for a governmental subsidy, perhaps reducing
or rebating the tax on low-income people,

00:50:23.880 --> 00:50:29.839
and perhaps having some type of guaranteed
minimum or negative income tax, and building

00:50:29.839 --> 00:50:34.730
a double-decker system in which you'd give
X to everybody out of general revenue somewhat

00:50:34.730 --> 00:50:41.380
like Mr. Friedman suggests, plus a variable
benefit related to the contributions you make.

00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:46.420
There are a number of such possibilities,
and in my paper I've indicated that all of

00:50:46.420 --> 00:50:52.500
those are possible as changes in the social
security system keeping the present general

00:50:52.500 --> 00:50:53.970
purpose and general direction.

00:50:53.970 --> 00:50:58.089
Milton Friedman: Now, I just want to ask one
question to Mr. Cohen in this connection.

00:50:58.089 --> 00:51:02.089
He has been emphasizing the extent to which
you can project the past into the future.

00:51:02.089 --> 00:51:09.550
Does he also project that just as in the past
20 years, the tax per person has gone up twice

00:51:09.550 --> 00:51:13.990
as fast as the benefit per person that that
will also continue because I think that's

00:51:13.990 --> 00:51:16.930
very highly relevant to the question that
was just asked?

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:22.849
We've had tax per covered person has risen
seven and a half fold from 50 to 70, and the

00:51:22.849 --> 00:51:25.119
benefit paid has risen something like threefold.

00:51:25.119 --> 00:51:27.570
Do you think that that discrepancy will continue?

00:51:27.570 --> 00:51:28.570
Mr. Cohen: Well, I don't know.

00:51:28.570 --> 00:51:33.050
Of course during the same time, marginal tax
rates for people in higher income groups have

00:51:33.050 --> 00:51:36.300
been reduced from 90% to 70%.

00:51:36.300 --> 00:51:40.260
Maybe what we have to do is increase that
70% to 80 or 90.

00:51:40.260 --> 00:51:46.059
Under your proposals for more general revenues,
you are pushing for higher marginal rates

00:51:46.059 --> 00:51:49.210
on higher income people, that's what your
proposals are leading to.

00:51:49.210 --> 00:51:52.180
Now, that's not what you favor but that's
what you're gonna get.

00:51:52.180 --> 00:51:53.180
Milton Friedman: I'm sorry...

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:56.380
It's not what I favor and it's not I hope
what I get and let me emphasize that what

00:51:56.380 --> 00:52:00.990
you just stated about the income tax is not
correct because you are looking at nominal

00:52:00.990 --> 00:52:03.000
rates and not at real rates.

00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:10.620
Inflation has raised the burden of the income
tax so that despite decreases in rates, the

00:52:10.620 --> 00:52:16.960
average effective rate paid on the personal
income tax is higher today than at any time

00:52:16.960 --> 00:52:20.349
in our history including the peak date in
World War Two.

00:52:20.349 --> 00:52:22.400
Mr. Lisagor: Question over there.

00:52:22.400 --> 00:52:25.329
Man: I'd like to ask Mr. Cohen, please sir.

00:52:25.329 --> 00:52:33.920
How do you ascertain the assertion, the fact,
that private initiative and private pension

00:52:33.920 --> 00:52:38.630
funds are greater because of, or in spite
of social security?

00:52:38.630 --> 00:52:42.530
You said that they are growing, how do you
know they wouldn't have grown twice as fast

00:52:42.530 --> 00:52:43.880
if you hadn't had social security?

00:52:43.880 --> 00:52:47.630
Wilbur Cohen: They would have, I would agree
they would have had to grow twice as fast

00:52:47.630 --> 00:52:51.160
if there weren't social security because the
social need would have been there.

00:52:51.160 --> 00:52:52.400
I do not deny that.

00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:54.920
Man: That has been a deterrent to the growth
of private funds.

00:52:54.920 --> 00:52:59.900
Wilbur Cohen: Well, certainly it's been a deterrent,
thank God because if you would have all of

00:52:59.900 --> 00:53:06.839
this money in private pension plans at the
present time with no vesting and with the

00:53:06.839 --> 00:53:11.790
sort of things you have in present private
plans, and Studebaker companies going bankrupt

00:53:11.790 --> 00:53:16.190
leaving people without pension rights, you'd
have disaster in the United States.

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:21.680
So I believe that the blend of social security
which is a minimum guarantee plus the selective

00:53:21.680 --> 00:53:26.530
private pension for companies, and unions,
and comported is the best of both possible

00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:27.530
worlds.

00:53:27.530 --> 00:53:32.740
Mr. Lisagor: Gentlemen we have time for one
brief question and hopefully a brief answer.

00:53:32.740 --> 00:53:33.740
The final.

00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:35.550
Wilbur Cohen: Hope springs eternal.

00:53:37.430 --> 00:53:44.880
Professor Friedman you have been preaching
the gospel of consumer sovereignty and I am

00:53:44.880 --> 00:53:50.890
fully in agreement with you but I'm just wondering
why is this contrary to the gospel of capitalism

00:53:50.890 --> 00:53:58.010
and freedom, that this consumer sovereignty,
if individual let's say goes out and buys

00:53:58.010 --> 00:54:03.290
monetary history of the United States, or
whatever way he wants to spend his money.

00:54:03.290 --> 00:54:09.099
But it is not consumer sovereignty when there
are a number of individuals band together

00:54:09.099 --> 00:54:16.450
and decide that they want to have social security
or some kind of benefits paid by the government,

00:54:16.450 --> 00:54:20.690
and for which they want to contribute or for
which they are going to get at some future

00:54:20.690 --> 00:54:21.690
date.

00:54:21.690 --> 00:54:28.170
But do you have some special knowledge that
this is contrary to the will of the electorate

00:54:28.170 --> 00:54:29.170
of the United States?

00:54:29.170 --> 00:54:33.630
Milton Friedman: Well, I think you've asked not
a simple question but an extremely complicated

00:54:33.630 --> 00:54:37.290
question that goes to basic political philosophy.

00:54:37.290 --> 00:54:43.470
In the first place, I have not been in any
way saying that we should violate the laws

00:54:43.470 --> 00:54:46.230
of the land or that the law should be decided
in another way.

00:54:46.230 --> 00:54:49.849
I've been trying to persuade the people if
they ought to vote differently.

00:54:49.849 --> 00:54:54.099
That's a trying to serve the same function
with respect to consumer sovereignty as you

00:54:54.099 --> 00:55:01.880
define it as advertising serves with respect
to consumer sovereignty in the private market.

00:55:01.880 --> 00:55:07.089
But much more fundamentally, I believe there
is a philosophical difference between consumer

00:55:07.089 --> 00:55:13.940
sovereignty in which 100% of the people decide
to join in a program and a situation in which

00:55:13.940 --> 00:55:19.099
51% of the people impose taxes on the other
49% of the people.

00:55:19.099 --> 00:55:24.020
I don't believe they really come under the
same rubric, and I think you do not agree...you

00:55:24.020 --> 00:55:25.650
do not believe so either.

00:55:25.650 --> 00:55:31.020
If I said to you, under your definition of
consumer sovereignty, if 51% of the people

00:55:31.020 --> 00:55:36.010
vote to shoot the other 49% of the people,
is that appropriate consumer sovereignty?

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:37.470
You would say no, of course not.

00:55:37.470 --> 00:55:39.950
Man: I would say it's not an appropriate similarity.

00:55:39.950 --> 00:55:44.609
Milton Friedman: Well, but exactly the same way
it is not an appropriate similarity to individual

00:55:44.609 --> 00:55:51.020
consumer sovereignty to say that 51% of the
people vote to impose taxes on 100% of the

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:55.180
people in order to pay benefits, let us say
to 100% of the people.

00:55:55.180 --> 00:56:01.570
I favor a majority rule as an expedient, as
a way in which there are certain decisions

00:56:01.570 --> 00:56:05.650
have to be made jointly and this is the least
bad way of doing it.

00:56:05.650 --> 00:56:07.910
But I do not regard it as a principle.

00:56:07.910 --> 00:56:14.940
Whereas I do regard individual responsibility
for himself that my using my resources to

00:56:14.940 --> 00:56:18.930
achieve my objectives so long as I don't interfere
with anybody else doing the same.

00:56:18.930 --> 00:56:21.319
I regard that as a principle.

00:56:21.319 --> 00:56:24.270
There are wholly different kinds of majority
rule as an expedient.

00:56:24.270 --> 00:56:25.950
Peter Lisagor: Mr. Cohen, a final comment.

00:56:25.950 --> 00:56:31.319
Wilbur Cohen: Individual responsibility may be
defined as a responsibility of people to have

00:56:31.319 --> 00:56:33.990
the freedom to starve if they wish.

00:56:33.990 --> 00:56:39.059
I believe that there's a higher and more noble
principle and that is a social responsibility

00:56:39.059 --> 00:56:44.859
of all society, when as Mr. Friedman says
the commandments say, "Honor thy father and

00:56:44.859 --> 00:56:50.490
mother," it did not say honor thy father and
thy mother as economists have predetermined.

00:56:50.490 --> 00:56:55.480
It said, "Honor thy father and thy mother
in all of the ways that you may think of,"

00:56:55.480 --> 00:56:59.650
including social responsibility and that is
what social security is.

00:56:59.650 --> 00:57:09.160
Mr Lisagor: Gentlemen, on that note I want
to thank both of you.

00:57:09.160 --> 00:57:15.720
I want to thank both of you who I think have
shown us what debate means and also what chutzpah

00:57:15.720 --> 00:57:17.770
means.

00:57:17.770 --> 00:57:21.920
And I want to thank you ladies and gentlemen
for your active participation.

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:38.060
Peter Hackes: Quite obviously, this will not bring an end to the discussion

00:57:38.069 --> 00:57:41.339
of the pros and cons of social security.

00:57:41.339 --> 00:57:46.070
Our focus of course has been to bring forth
some pertinent views in this field.

00:57:46.070 --> 00:57:51.090
The American Enterprise Institute has been
pleased to present arguments on both sides.

00:57:51.090 --> 00:57:58.400
This is Peter Hackett in Washington.

00:57:58.400 --> 00:58:04.680
Narrator: Washington debates for the '70s
is created and supplied to this station as

00:58:04.680 --> 00:58:27.500
a public service by the American Enterprise
Institute, Washington, D.C.

